n    patterns of comparison and contrast —\n                           e using a comparative form of an adjective:\n                                 I think it’s more important to have COMfortable  clothes &  than STYlish ones @.\n\n                                 Notice that starting the falling or (fall-)rising tone on different words can affect meaning:\n                                 watching  FOOTball   69 is much better than watching CRICKet   &.\n                                 WATCHing    football &  is much better than PLAYing  football\n                           e using either ... or:\n                                 You  can either catch the EARlier train     or the LATer one &.\n                                 I’ve either left my wallet at HOME       or     LOST  it &.\n                           e using ..., mot... or...\n                                 He’s got bronCHItis  &,  not just a COUGH\n                                 I'm not really ANgry with  him       just a bit aaNOYED\n                           e using other contrasting phrases (e.g. catch the bus versus walk home; reducing the cost of\n                             public transport versus increasing it):\n                                 Rather  than catch the BUS       maybe  we  could WALK   home  @.\n                                 We  should be reDUcing  the cost of public transport & instead of inCREASing  it @.\n\n\n                 98           English Pronunciation in Use (Advanced)", "char_count": 3436, "mean_conf": 89.97, "word_count": 359, "words": [{"text": "!", "x0": 99, "y0": 213, "x1": 101, "y1": 220, "conf": 30, "bold": false}, {"text": "AB", "x0": 205, "y0": 124, "x1": 435, "y1": 276, "conf": 93, "bold": false}, {"text": "Although", "x0": 510, "y0": 132, "x1": 849, "y1": 216, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "|", "x0": 891, "y0": 132, "x1": 901, "y1": 196, "conf": 88, "bold": false}, {"text": "was", "x0": 940, "y0": 151, "x1": 1075, "y1": 196, "conf": 96, "b